#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124206 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124624 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "86517de9-0e7c-5c00-a3e8-7aeccbf12d9b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Global Maximum of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "30557b46-f1f0-51dc-94ed-5d84515bc4b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-1112" :question/problem "What is the global maximum point of the function shown below?\n\n![](images/9a/9a6d6716f8187ca4ba8e3a43bce086f89f92eff4b13968a8278187832542a90d.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global maximum of a function is the largest value that the function attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global maximum.\n\n- A global maximum point is a point $(x,y)$ where a function reaches a global maximum. There can be zero, one, or many global maximum points.\n\nFor our function, the maximum value reached by the function is $f(x)=4.$ Therefore, the global maximum is $4.$ The function reaches this value when $x=-1,$ so the global maximum point is $(-1,4).$ \n\n![](images/f6/f6dec47fd3608523aeca83aac92b87407c3892598bf94e7bce14ae05881cb077.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124207 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124625 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9f1c88f-1c8a-52f9-a144-b52f67564f5e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether the Global Extrema of a Function Exist"}] :question/id #uuid "c7c94a49-1a4e-51f6-b0e7-213c67f40fd9" :question/math-academy-id "e-1113" :question/problem "The graph $y = f(x)$ is shown below. Which of the following statements is true? \n\nI. The global maximum of $f(x)$ is $1.$\n\nII. The global minimum of $f(x)$ is $-4.$\n\nIII. The function does not have any global extrema.\n\n![](images/ce/ceb091d2752790facfd1f63acd772f19b2f1f5808e2e90e6813f793831008bf7.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. As $x$ increases on the right-hand side of the graph, the function continues to increase without bound (as indicated by the arrow). So, the function does not reach a global maximum value.\n\n- Statement II is false. At first glance, it might seem that the point $(-4,-4)$ corresponds to the global minimum, but since $x=-4$ is not included in the domain (as indicated by the open circle), the function does *not* have a global minimum.\n\n- Statement III is true. The function does not have a global maximum and does not have a global minimum. Therefore, the function does not have any global extrema.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124372 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124564 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ec3d139-c43a-5547-b0ae-147e41a55180" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Global Minimum of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "c844db9d-a556-5ce4-90e8-2db1b5671579" :question/math-academy-id "e-6684" :question/problem "What are the global minimum points of the function shown below?\n\n![](images/9a/9a6d6716f8187ca4ba8e3a43bce086f89f92eff4b13968a8278187832542a90d.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global minimum of a function is the smallest value that the function attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global minimum.\n\n- A global minimum point of a function is a point $(x,y)$ where a function reaches a global minimum. There can be zero, one, or many global minimum points.\n\nFor our function, the minimum value reached by the function is $f(x)=0.$ Therefore, the global minimum is $0.$ The function reaches this value when $x=-3$ and when $x=1,$ so  the global minimum points are $(-3,0)$ and $(1,0).$\n\n![](images/41/413c4481cba4ee02fef831deb801f171b90f125937ac51e3ed283a5d94ccc5d7.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125032 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124625 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9f1c88f-1c8a-52f9-a144-b52f67564f5e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether the Global Extrema of a Function Exist"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5ce4a47-4847-52bf-892f-c108c51fca51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122793} {:answer/id #uuid "c83506ce-154a-557b-9048-857b59a28bf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122794} {:answer/id #uuid "cdf5078d-cd71-5f47-b8e2-d29511ecf06e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122795} {:answer/id #uuid "d3f04c76-f68c-564d-b49d-b7474db9a0b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122796} {:answer/id #uuid "55073095-1340-53cb-aa81-7253d5b74204" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122797}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c83506ce-154a-557b-9048-857b59a28bf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122794} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ddafb7b-0d59-5318-975b-55322afb08c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122792}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "742f04e6-7de9-5ed2-b2c9-aed94e2c10f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-37140" :question/problem "![](images/b5/b57f5f955178e8d10fff22c92d6cab0d009e4243ee90d0c48c36a67673316ae2.png)\n\nThe graph $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. The global maximum of $f(x)$ is $4.$\n\nII. The global minimum of $f(x)$ is $1.$\n\nIII. The function does not have any global extrema." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. At first glance, it might seem that the point $(1,4)$ corresponds to the global maximum, but since $x=1$ is not included in the domain (as indicated by the open circle), the function does *not* have a global maximum.\n\n- Statement II is true. The global minimum point is $(5,1),$ and so the minimum value reached by the function is $f(x)=1.$ Therefore, the global minimum is $1.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The function $f(x)$ has a global minimum at the point $(5,1),$ and a global minimum is a type of global extremum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125099 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124564 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ec3d139-c43a-5547-b0ae-147e41a55180" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Global Minimum of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "067d652b-9f0d-5613-a90b-b0e3b721c99e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123195} {:answer/id #uuid "2dcb85a8-fd17-51b8-b10b-27f0d337fde3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123196} {:answer/id #uuid "61ae9e75-6941-5ba5-8b0e-4ccddc7559e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123197} {:answer/id #uuid "63620a8e-c435-5e51-b373-9c326fcf810c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123198} {:answer/id #uuid "7a246617-303c-5660-86bd-4280d6f5e010" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123199}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2dcb85a8-fd17-51b8-b10b-27f0d337fde3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123196} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7fe688e-436c-5cc3-a9c9-bb187008c36b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123194}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cbe82116-66d1-5999-b7fe-93f60e189fc0" :question/math-academy-id "q-37783" :question/problem "![](images/db/db99cf19eaba74238a307b280f06ef8cbf17a08ada86b293282105edd7281edb.png)\n\nWhat is the global minimum of the function shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global minimum of a function is the smallest value that the function attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global minimum.\n\n- A global minimum point of a function is a point $(x,y)$ where a function reaches a global minimum. There can be zero, one, or many global minimum points.\n\nThe minimum value reached by the function is $f(x)=-1.5.$ Therefore, the global minimum is $-1.5.$ The function reaches this value at both $x=-2$ and $x=2.$\n\n![](images/a2/a262ec40b439cceec6be37da8d590588012a0c3b9eed676dd09d0673540541b1.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125100 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124625 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9f1c88f-1c8a-52f9-a144-b52f67564f5e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether the Global Extrema of a Function Exist"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d41e3945-aa5d-5d22-b461-bfcb2958883f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123201} {:answer/id #uuid "eafb17b5-bce3-5d7b-a0c8-21605d552203" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123202} {:answer/id #uuid "027a2f55-6660-5e08-a474-e37e0d300bde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123203} {:answer/id #uuid "205a855c-8eea-5606-9aac-74123fc927a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123204} {:answer/id #uuid "2f74cab4-a056-5487-a356-a0466c8a7545" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "205a855c-8eea-5606-9aac-74123fc927a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123204} :answer-field/id #uuid "47fd133d-f916-5288-a89d-e8641086ec21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123200}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7352c6aa-8892-5912-9137-9515fbb584d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-37793" :question/problem "![](images/f2/f27757e4cfa1a17c436e7f99e05101f8596b53d7fea6add643ab32da4eb4f737.png)\n\nThe graph $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements is true? \n\nI. The global maximum of $f(x)$ is $2.$\n\nII. The global minimum of $f(x)$ is $-2.$\n\nIII. The function does not have any global extrema." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. As $x$ increases on the right-hand side or decreases on the left-hand side of the graph, the function continues to increase without bound (as indicated by the two arrows). So, the function does not reach a global maximum value.\n\n- Statement II is false. The global minimum points are $(-2,-1)$ and $(2,-1).$ So the minimum value reached by the function is $f(x)=-1.$ Therefore, the global minimum is $-1.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The function $f(x)$ has global minimums at the points $(-2,-1)$ and $(2,-1),$ and a global minimum is a type of global extremum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125176 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124624 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "86517de9-0e7c-5c00-a3e8-7aeccbf12d9b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Global Maximum of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9003d3c5-dcd6-508b-ad33-ae215695168b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123657} {:answer/id #uuid "d8d63bc9-8723-588d-92b4-7be95eeebff7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123659} {:answer/id #uuid "3cb13f4a-d6d7-5fb9-a460-a2eba1eceb93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-0.5,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123660} {:answer/id #uuid "611bdd25-995b-53c8-9458-1053f746c097" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123661} {:answer/id #uuid "d87813cd-9639-536b-8606-d16f473daed5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507142}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d87813cd-9639-536b-8606-d16f473daed5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507142} :answer-field/id #uuid "d90d2bf0-e240-53eb-b7ad-088d87d8cfab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123656}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "74aad1ab-48f5-51e2-9d34-4d5cb358db5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-60753" :question/problem "![](images/26/2698a97b97bdcf7bc2787abce5f9b9d66c5b56b1f0b106f54eab534666fd62c2.png)\n\nWhat is the global maximum point of the function shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global maximum of a function is the largest value that the function attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global maximum.\n\n- A global maximum point is a point $(x,y)$ where a function reaches a global maximum. There can be zero, one, or many global maximum points.\n\nFor our function, the maximum value reached by the function is $f(x)=1.$ Therefore, the global maximum is $1.$ The function reaches this value when $x=2,$ so the global maximum point is $(2,1).$ \n\n![](images/ef/ef7ce52349abebebe9b1cfd210a611aac821aff01ae8c05925daa1540786e887.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507446 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124624 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "86517de9-0e7c-5c00-a3e8-7aeccbf12d9b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Global Maximum of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "225e8ee8-107a-534f-b4c8-02ed8c43d591" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(8,14)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506750} {:answer/id #uuid "2bb34db4-21fa-598d-9927-dbc4625fd148" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(12,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506781} {:answer/id #uuid "5c982468-395e-5ab6-80d8-d436514480b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506899} {:answer/id #uuid "897d451d-9cf4-5c17-9220-e1869043bddc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506983} {:answer/id #uuid "e700fab0-ad95-55d4-a6d8-ebd38fee670c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-6,0)" :db/id #edb/re